'' THE TYPE-FIRST BITFIELD SPELLING LOST ITS WIDTH, AND WITH IT THE PACKING.
''
'' fbc accepts TWO spellings and rejects a third -- measured:
''
''     a : 3 as ulong      accepted
''     as ulong a : 3      accepted
''     a as ulong : 3      error 14, Expected identifier
''
'' Only the NAME-FIRST branch read the width. The type-first field came out an ORDINARY
'' field of its declared type, so nothing packed and every field took its own unit:
''
''     type BF : as ulong a : 3 : as ulong b : 5 : end type
''     print sizeof( BF )      fbc: 4      tiko: 8
''
'' A WRONG LAYOUT. The values read back correctly -- that is what hid it -- so what was
'' wrong was sizeof and the OFFSET OF EVERYTHING AFTER IT. A UDT matched against a C or
'' Win32 header is wrong at runtime with nothing said, and so is anything written to a
'' file or passed to an API.
''
'' The three-field TypeFirst is here because two fields can pack by accident into one
'' unit of the declared width; 3 + 5 + 24 is exactly 32 and cannot.
''
'' A ':' AFTER A FIELD IS NORMALLY A STATEMENT SEPARATOR -- 'as integer x : as integer y'
'' is two fields on one line, 16 bytes -- so the width is read only when a NUMBER
'' follows, and the position is put back when one does not.
''
'' Differenced against fbc: '4 4 16' and then '5 17 999'.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/bitfield-typefirst.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/bitfield-typefirst.out
